WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.402
In this lesson, we'll start by

2
00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:04.971
defining what an AI agent actually is.

3
00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:08.975
Then look at what happens when you interact with a stateless agent

4
00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.678
versus one augmented with memory.

5
00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:15.982
You will see why conversational history alone isn't enough

6
00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:19.052
and what agent memory really means.

7
00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:23.056
We'll wrap up by introducing the agent stack and a memory core,

8
00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:27.060
the architectural foundation that the rest of this course builds on.

9
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.529
Let's dive in.

10
00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:32.032
In this lesson, we're going to cover why AI agents need memory.

11
00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:34.234
More specifically, we're going to get an

12
00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:36.236
overview of what an AI agent is,

13
00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:39.839
what we mean by stateless agent and memory augmented agent,

14
00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:43.043
and we're also going to get an overview of conversational history

15
00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:45.545
and going beyond conversational history.

16
00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:48.648
We'll finalize this lesson by looking at two key concepts,

17
00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:50.850
which are Agent Memory and what

18
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.253
we mean by the Agent Memory Core.

19
00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:55.855
What is an AI Agent?

20
00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:57.957
An AI agent is a computational entity

21
00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:00.060
that can perceive its environment through inputs,

22
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.062
take action through tool use,

23
00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:05.965
and also has reasoning capabilities enabled by an LLM.

24
00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:10.670
And more importantly, an AI agent has some form of augmented memory,

25
00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.474
typically to allow it to store, retrieve, and apply knowledge

26
00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:16.576
across sessions and interactions.

27
00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.079
This is what we define as an AI agent.

28
00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:22.982
Generally, AI agents should be able to operate independently,

29
00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:26.986
act on behalf of the human with almost little to no feedback.

30
00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:29.989
Ideally, they are goal and objective bound.

31
00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:33.259
Now that we have an understanding of what an AI agent is,

32
00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.962
it's time for us to get a very clear understanding

33
00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:38.264
of the importance of memory for AI agents.

34
00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.266
We'll do this by looking at how

35
00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.669
an AI agent would operate without memory.

36
00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:46.473
Typically you would have an AI agent embedded within an application.

37
00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:50.777
And in this application, a user would interact with the agent.

38
00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:55.181
Typically, we can imagine a user asking for recommendations

39
00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:57.183
for restaurants around the area.

40
00:01:57.584 --> 00:01:59.385
We would refer to this as turn 1.

41
00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.888
This message would then be sent to the agent.

42
00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:04.190
And the agent would generate a response,

43
00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.593
ideally detecting the intent of the user,

44
00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:09.295
reasoning about the message the user sent,

45
00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:12.866
and using a bunch of tools to actually achieve the objective,

46
00:02:12.866 --> 00:02:14.968
such as searching for location

47
00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:17.370
and getting restaurants around a location,

48
00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:20.607
before finally providing an output to the user.

49
00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:24.010
We can refer to this as interaction turns.

50
00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:27.614
Following subsequent turns, we would have the user

51
00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:31.317
interact again with the same agent to continue the conversation.

52
00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.620
We can refer to this as turn 3.

53
00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.789
When the user gets the recommendations from the agent,

54
00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:38.892
the user could possibly ask

55
00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:41.895
to book the first recommendation on the list.

56
00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:44.097
But an agent without memory

57
00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:46.599
would actually respond as shown on the screen,

58
00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:48.968
which would be it has no recollection

59
00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.871
of the conversation the user is referring to.

60
00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.841
and would ask the user to please specify.

61
00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:57.143
We could refer to this as turn 4.

62
00:02:57.544 --> 00:03:00.413
In this event, we can see that due to lack of memory,

63
00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.649
the agent is unable to complete

64
00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:05.118
the task that the user has specified.

65
00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:08.521
We can refer to this as a multi-turn interaction.

66
00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.024
But obviously, this is without memory.

67
00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.693
Now we've seen what an AI agent

68
00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:16.796
with just Perception, Action, and Reasoning characteristics

69
00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:19.299
would operate like in a real-life scenario.

70
00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.901
Essentially, we would refer to this

71
00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.270
type of AI agent as a stateless agent.

72
00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:27.574
Let's talk more about what a stateless agent is.

73
00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:30.677
A stateless agent is one that can actually

74
00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:33.179
still perceive its environment through input

75
00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.481
and reason over this input and produce outputs

76
00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.684
back to the user, essentially

77
00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:39.886
powered by an LLM.

78
00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:42.455
And this feedback is very crucial.

79
00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:46.259
But one thing is, with no memory, as we've seen,

80
00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:49.062
the AI agent doesn't retain or recall information

81
00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:51.064
beyond a single turn.

82
00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:54.000
There are significant disadvantages to this.

83
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.469
For example, the agent will not

84
00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:58.805
be able to complete a long-horizon task.

85
00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:02.008
Now, these are tasks where the agent is expected to run

86
00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:04.711
for several minutes to hours or even days.

87
00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:09.382
But without having information of previous interaction or steps taken,

88
00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:11.985
it would be very difficult to complete the task.

89
00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:16.389
The agent will also have no context awareness across sessions,

90
00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:19.592
meaning if a user was to interact with an agent

91
00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:22.395
and then leave and come back or start a new session,

92
00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:25.398
the information as to the user would be lost

93
00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.600
and not transferred across sessions.

94
00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:31.404
More importantly, the agent would lack adaptation capabilities,

95
00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:34.707
which means any new information provided to the agent

96
00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.377
during an interaction would not be updated

97
00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.979
or used in subsequent interactions.

98
00:04:40.747 --> 00:04:44.450
Stateless agent can actually have high operational cost

99
00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:47.854
because to keep or at least augment continuity,

100
00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:50.523
you would have to stuff a lot of information

101
00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.925
into the context window at every single turn.

102
00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:56.929
There are more disadvantages, but these are the significant ones.

103
00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.333
Now, we've had an overview of stateless agent,

104
00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:02.335
which is an agent without memory.

105
00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:04.771
But we can move on and see what

106
00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:06.639
an AI agent with memory would look like.

107
00:05:07.073 --> 00:05:10.910
We're going to use a similar scenario as to the previous one.

108
00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:12.912
But let's start from turn 3.

109
00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:16.349
Turn 1 and 2 in a Memory-Augmented Agent

110
00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:18.418
would have been stored in an

111
00:05:18.418 --> 00:05:20.586
external memory source such as a database.

112
00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.324
So when we start with the user interacting with the application

113
00:05:24.624 --> 00:05:26.726
and also the agent indirectly,

114
00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:29.028
we would have turn one and two

115
00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:31.130
within the memory of the agent.

116
00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:36.336
What this means is, when the user asks for some recommendations

117
00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:38.237
and also asks them to book the

118
00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.139
recommendation on the first on the list.

119
00:05:40.206 --> 00:05:43.009
The agent will respond appropriately,

120
00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:47.480
which is identify the restaurant on the first on the list

121
00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.083
and actually provide a reasonable output,

122
00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:52.285
which would be asking the user

123
00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:55.688
what date and time would the booking like to be made for.

124
00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:58.758
We can refer to both turn 1 and 2

125
00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:01.561
and turn 3 and 4 as interaction history.

126
00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:04.163
And all of this will be stored in a database

127
00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:06.165
for all subsequent turns.

128
00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:10.670
Essentially, we have a memory-augmented agent.

129
00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:15.937
Let's take a further deep dive into Memory-Augmented Agents and their advantages.

130
00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:19.445
Just like stateless agents, they can Perceive Inputs

131
00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:23.449
and also Reason Over Inputs and Produce Outputs.

132
00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:27.787
All of this will be powered by the LLM reasoning capabilities,

133
00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:30.189
but a more important thing here is

134
00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:33.993
we have a database where information is stored and retrieved from.

135
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.363
There are many benefits to this, such as the agent's

136
00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:40.366
being able to complete long-horizon tasks,

137
00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:43.870
has subsequent context awareness and can actually adapt.

138
00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.773
Let's go over the advantages in more detail.

139
00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:51.144
One of the key advantages is the ability for memory-augmented agents

140
00:06:51.144 --> 00:06:52.845
to actually complete long-horizon tasks,

141
00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:56.149
mainly because they can reference previous interaction and context

142
00:06:56.149 --> 00:06:57.850
held in previous sessions.

143
00:06:58.217 --> 00:07:01.721
Now this leads to a Sustained Context Awareness,

144
00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:03.756
which feels to the user that there

145
00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:06.125
is a continuous interaction had with the agent.

146
00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:10.029
Now, what we have ideally is an improved sense of efficiency

147
00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:12.131
and also reduce operational cost.

148
00:07:12.131 --> 00:07:14.200
This is because we reduce the amount of

149
00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.502
information we have to pass into the context window

150
00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:18.604
and only pass in one that are

151
00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:21.007
relevant to the interaction that have been stored

152
00:07:21.007 --> 00:07:23.209
in an external memory store.

153
00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:28.247
Finally, we'll have greater reliability in multi-step workflow.

154
00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:30.650
This is mainly due to the fact that

155
00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.518
we can reference previous steps that were taken

156
00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:35.221
and also reference previous context,

157
00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:38.524
which makes all the subsequent steps more reliable

158
00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.726
in terms of successful outcomes.

159
00:07:41.194 --> 00:07:43.396
We're going to be exploring more of

160
00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:45.598
all of these advantages in future lessons.

161
00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:49.202
Memory augmented agents come in all shape and sizes.

162
00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:53.005
What we've seen might be a naive form of memory augmented agent.

163
00:07:53.206 --> 00:07:55.308
And here's what we mean. Going from

164
00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:57.710
a stateless agent to one that can remember

165
00:07:57.977 --> 00:08:01.681
means storing interaction history within an external store.

166
00:08:02.014 --> 00:08:04.217
The key benefits of this we've touched on,

167
00:08:04.217 --> 00:08:07.820
but the most important one is the continuation of interaction.

168
00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:11.524
Essentially, we're bringing continuity to an agent

169
00:08:11.524 --> 00:08:15.228
through the storing of conversational history or interactions.

170
00:08:15.261 --> 00:08:18.764
And these interactions are typically between the user or users

171
00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:21.033
or the agents or assistants.

172
00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:24.437
Interaction history, when it's stored in an external store,

173
00:08:24.437 --> 00:08:26.739
can be referred to as Conversational Memory.

174
00:08:26.739 --> 00:08:29.642
We're going to be looking more into different forms of memory,

175
00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:33.045
but conversational memory is one of the simplest forms to understand

176
00:08:33.346 --> 00:08:36.249
one that interaction history lends itself to.

177
00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:39.552
An interaction is typically back and forth information

178
00:08:39.652 --> 00:08:41.854
between a user and an agent.

179
00:08:42.288 --> 00:08:46.025
In conversational memory, we have very specific attributes.

180
00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:49.095
We have a timestamp, which is when the interaction was taken.

181
00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:51.931
We have the user and the assistant message,

182
00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:55.668
and this will be stored in a database, in external memory.

183
00:08:56.402 --> 00:08:58.871
Ideally, conversational memory will be time-ordered,

184
00:08:59.272 --> 00:09:03.175
which means that when we return any conversational memory data,

185
00:09:03.576 --> 00:09:05.578
we are retrieving it ordered by time,

186
00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:09.549
so we can see the sequence of actions and interactions taken.

187
00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:11.918
Let's see how this would look like

188
00:09:11.918 --> 00:09:13.819
in the context window of an LLM.

189
00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.822
So an LLM would have a context window

190
00:09:16.856 --> 00:09:18.925
that can take a certain amount of tokens.

191
00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:21.327
In the context window, we would

192
00:09:21.327 --> 00:09:23.329
place in the system prompt and instructions.

193
00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:27.333
Now, interaction history will be retrieved as conversational memory.

194
00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:32.238
where we'll put all the multi-turn past interaction from the external store.

195
00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:35.841
And then we'll place in the final user prompt.

196
00:09:36.142 --> 00:09:39.245
This is a depiction of what the context window

197
00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:42.548
of an LLM with conversational memory would look like.

198
00:09:43.349 --> 00:09:45.351
But we actually need to go beyond this.

199
00:09:45.651 --> 00:09:47.320
And there are several reasons as to

200
00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.221
why we need to move beyond conversational memory

201
00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:51.223
or just using interaction history

202
00:09:51.257 --> 00:09:53.826
to create memory augmented agents.

203
00:09:54.327 --> 00:09:56.329
The first one is very simple.

204
00:09:56.329 --> 00:09:58.631
Conversation windows are actually finite,

205
00:09:59.031 --> 00:10:00.933
but the user relationships are not.

206
00:10:01.334 --> 00:10:03.636
What we mean is that we can actually capture

207
00:10:03.636 --> 00:10:06.939
more relationships between users and the assistant

208
00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:09.041
by looking at conversations

209
00:10:09.041 --> 00:10:11.644
or other data associated with conversations.

210
00:10:11.944 --> 00:10:14.347
For example, information as to entities

211
00:10:14.347 --> 00:10:16.148
that are mentioned during an interaction.

212
00:10:16.482 --> 00:10:20.987
entities such as place, people, and relationships to people.

213
00:10:21.487 --> 00:10:25.391
Not all valuable information are actually stored in a single conversation.

214
00:10:25.992 --> 00:10:28.294
We're going to have to move beyond conversational memory

215
00:10:28.694 --> 00:10:31.297
to actually extract useful information

216
00:10:31.998 --> 00:10:35.001
that we can use in cross-session interaction.

217
00:10:35.501 --> 00:10:39.505
Agents need structured, queryable knowledge, not just chat logs.

218
00:10:39.839 --> 00:10:41.941
Data stored in conversation memory

219
00:10:41.941 --> 00:10:43.943
is just interaction history.

220
00:10:44.343 --> 00:10:46.445
But agents can operate within workflow

221
00:10:46.445 --> 00:10:48.147
where the steps taken in the workflow

222
00:10:48.147 --> 00:10:51.350
is actually useful information and also the outcomes.

223
00:10:51.651 --> 00:10:55.154
Now, this is not conversation history or interaction history.

224
00:10:55.454 --> 00:10:58.157
Therefore, we need to expand and go beyond conversational memory,

225
00:10:58.457 --> 00:11:00.459
which we will see in future lessons.

226
00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.829
Now that we've seen conversational memory,

227
00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:07.433
it's time to explore the distinct forms of agent memory

228
00:11:07.433 --> 00:11:09.135
you will be coming across.

229
00:11:09.535 --> 00:11:12.438
The easy way to view agent memory

230
00:11:12.438 --> 00:11:14.640
or in two distinct form, which are short-term and long-term.

231
00:11:15.107 --> 00:11:17.209
Let's look into short-term memory.

232
00:11:17.309 --> 00:11:19.712
Two common forms of short-term memory

233
00:11:19.712 --> 00:11:21.414
are Semantic Cache and Working Memory.

234
00:11:21.714 --> 00:11:25.117
Semantic Cache is essentially using a caching mechanisms

235
00:11:25.117 --> 00:11:27.119
that leverage vector search

236
00:11:27.119 --> 00:11:31.123
and previously received response from an inference provider

237
00:11:31.424 --> 00:11:34.026
to essentially use as response

238
00:11:34.026 --> 00:11:36.929
for similar queries in subsequent interaction.

239
00:11:37.196 --> 00:11:39.699
While Working Memory can be seen as

240
00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:43.402
the LLM Context Window and any Session Based memory.

241
00:11:43.703 --> 00:11:46.005
This is essentially seen as a scratch pad

242
00:11:46.105 --> 00:11:48.107
where the LLM can operate within

243
00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:51.711
but is lost after an interaction or session.

244
00:11:52.144 --> 00:11:55.047
These are all the different types of short-term memory.

245
00:11:55.347 --> 00:11:58.250
For long-term memory, we have three main forms,

246
00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:01.654
such as Procedural, Semantic, and Episodic.

247
00:12:02.054 --> 00:12:04.857
Let's look at examples of these types of memory.

248
00:12:05.658 --> 00:12:08.627
For Procedural memory, a common memory type

249
00:12:08.861 --> 00:12:12.231
we're going to be using in agents is Workflow memory.

250
00:12:12.531 --> 00:12:15.034
This is where we store the steps and interaction

251
00:12:15.034 --> 00:12:18.437
that an agent has taken to achieve its subsequent objective.

252
00:12:18.738 --> 00:12:23.442
And these steps could include the calling of tools and other processes.

253
00:12:23.542 --> 00:12:26.946
It's ideal to record the steps in a form of external memory,

254
00:12:26.946 --> 00:12:30.449
which can be referenced and pulled in in subsequent interaction

255
00:12:30.549 --> 00:12:33.853
as a form of experience for the agent to refer to.

256
00:12:34.153 --> 00:12:36.255
We call this Workflow memory.

257
00:12:36.555 --> 00:12:40.159
A good example of Semantic memory is simply the Knowledge Base,

258
00:12:40.459 --> 00:12:43.329
which is any external knowledge that the agent needs to know

259
00:12:43.329 --> 00:12:46.732
to complete a task. This could be domain-specific knowledge

260
00:12:47.032 --> 00:12:49.835
in regards to the domain that the agent operates within.

261
00:12:50.336 --> 00:12:53.038
Then conversation memory that we explored previously

262
00:12:53.239 --> 00:12:55.341
is known as a type of Episodic memory.

263
00:12:55.708 --> 00:12:59.211
typically because we have the timestamp attribute

264
00:12:59.211 --> 00:13:01.480
on the information captured

265
00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:05.684
and we could reference this specific data using time.

266
00:13:06.252 --> 00:13:10.256
Now we have a good understanding of different types of agent memory.

267
00:13:10.756 --> 00:13:14.059
It's time to understand what specifically is agent memory.

268
00:13:15.094 --> 00:13:19.098
Agent memory can be defined as the composition of system component

269
00:13:19.665 --> 00:13:22.067
with some architectural components as well,

270
00:13:22.368 --> 00:13:25.538
in order to enable an agent to adapt and learn.

271
00:13:25.938 --> 00:13:29.742
The system components that you'll find in Agent Memory are typically

272
00:13:29.842 --> 00:13:32.745
Embedding Models, a database,

273
00:13:32.845 --> 00:13:35.047
and a large language model.

274
00:13:35.247 --> 00:13:38.150
Ideally, the combination of these system components,

275
00:13:38.751 --> 00:13:41.453
along with some control mechanisms and software harness,

276
00:13:41.453 --> 00:13:43.455
which is code, will enable an agent

277
00:13:44.056 --> 00:13:47.560
to store, retrieve, and recall information

278
00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:50.729
that allows it to adapt to interaction and learn.

279
00:13:51.463 --> 00:13:53.699
To understand agent memory,

280
00:13:53.799 --> 00:13:57.469
we can use your previous knowledge on retrieval augmented generation, or RAG.

281
00:13:58.003 --> 00:14:00.639
We're going to see how RAG works very quickly

282
00:14:00.739 --> 00:14:03.742
and then bring that in and connect it to agent memory.

283
00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:06.612
Let's go over a typical RAG pipeline.

284
00:14:06.979 --> 00:14:09.081
First you would have a data source

285
00:14:09.081 --> 00:14:11.684
which you will pass through a Data Processing pipeline,

286
00:14:11.684 --> 00:14:15.487
which will include the breaking down of the data object

287
00:14:15.487 --> 00:14:17.389
into subsequent chunks.

288
00:14:18.123 --> 00:14:20.926
These chunks are then passed into an embedding model,

289
00:14:21.026 --> 00:14:24.730
where a numerical representation of the data object is created.

290
00:14:25.130 --> 00:14:28.634
This numerical representation captures the semantics and context

291
00:14:28.734 --> 00:14:31.937
of the data object that was passed into the embedding model.

292
00:14:32.304 --> 00:14:35.507
Alongside with the Embedding Model and other metadata,

293
00:14:35.507 --> 00:14:38.010
we can pass in all different data

294
00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.512
types into a single database for storage.

295
00:14:41.113 --> 00:14:44.817
Now, we would have a user which will interact with an application

296
00:14:45.117 --> 00:14:47.419
by passing in a User Query,

297
00:14:47.519 --> 00:14:49.788
which would then be vectorized by passing

298
00:14:49.788 --> 00:14:52.391
in the User Query into the Embedding Model.

299
00:14:52.524 --> 00:14:56.362
and generating a numerical representation of the User Query.

300
00:14:56.929 --> 00:14:58.697
Typically, the Embedding Model used to

301
00:14:58.697 --> 00:15:00.799
generate the embedding for the User Query

302
00:15:00.799 --> 00:15:03.335
would be the same we use during the ingestion pipeline.

303
00:15:03.903 --> 00:15:07.706
Once we retrieve the semantically similar rows to the user query,

304
00:15:07.907 --> 00:15:10.409
we would pass these rows into a Reranking Model.

305
00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:13.612
The results of the reranked rows are then concatenated

306
00:15:13.612 --> 00:15:16.115
with the user query and passed into the LLM

307
00:15:16.715 --> 00:15:20.119
to ground the response of the LLM in domain-specific data.

308
00:15:20.352 --> 00:15:22.655
Now, this is a typical RAG pipeline.

309
00:15:23.555 --> 00:15:25.257
In order to make a connection

310
00:15:25.257 --> 00:15:27.259
to how RAG relates to agent memory,

311
00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:29.461
we will be taking the following steps.

312
00:15:30.062 --> 00:15:31.830
We would have the typical ingestion process

313
00:15:32.164 --> 00:15:34.133
in the earlier portion of a RAG pipeline.

314
00:15:34.833 --> 00:15:37.536
And we would also bring in our knowledge of an AI agent

315
00:15:37.836 --> 00:15:40.539
and the main characteristics which are the Perception,

316
00:15:40.839 --> 00:15:43.842
Memory, Action, and Reasoning capabilities

317
00:15:44.143 --> 00:15:46.111
that we covered previously. To make

318
00:15:46.111 --> 00:15:48.414
the connection of RAG to agent memory,

319
00:15:48.814 --> 00:15:52.718
what you would have is the abstractions or the memory types

320
00:15:52.718 --> 00:15:55.688
that we covered in previous slides

321
00:15:55.688 --> 00:15:58.691
as computational form within the database.

322
00:15:59.191 --> 00:16:02.394
Ideally, this would be tables within a database.

323
00:16:02.895 --> 00:16:04.997
So you'd have your semantic memory,

324
00:16:05.197 --> 00:16:07.800
your procedural memory, or episodic memory,

325
00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.102
which can be conversational memory,

326
00:16:10.102 --> 00:16:12.204
as tables represented in a database.

327
00:16:12.738 --> 00:16:14.940
A memory manager could then be used

328
00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:17.242
to abstract away the methods

329
00:16:17.242 --> 00:16:20.646
and program used to read, write, update

330
00:16:20.646 --> 00:16:23.549
and delete data from these tables.

331
00:16:23.916 --> 00:16:27.219
And our agent would have access to all of these capabilities

332
00:16:27.219 --> 00:16:30.422
by connecting a Memory Manager through tools

333
00:16:30.422 --> 00:16:33.125
and providing these as capabilities

334
00:16:33.125 --> 00:16:35.227
to the agent via memory.

335
00:16:35.794 --> 00:16:39.398
And this is how we bring in our previous knowledge of RAG

336
00:16:39.698 --> 00:16:42.701
and also the characteristics of an agent

337
00:16:43.002 --> 00:16:47.106
and different forms of memory into agent memory.

338
00:16:47.506 --> 00:16:51.310
Now, we're going to cover the term Agent Memory Core.

339
00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:54.880
In an agentic system, there are three main components

340
00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:58.684
where memory is located or could be said to be located.

341
00:16:58.717 --> 00:17:00.819
This would be your large language model,

342
00:17:00.819 --> 00:17:04.323
which has parametric memory of all the data it's been trained with.

343
00:17:04.523 --> 00:17:06.525
and also your Embedding Model,

344
00:17:06.525 --> 00:17:08.927
which has memory by which it could draw semantic

345
00:17:08.927 --> 00:17:12.131
and context information from when generating an embedding.

346
00:17:12.731 --> 00:17:14.733
But also you have your database.

347
00:17:15.034 --> 00:17:17.336
This is where you're going to see the most traffic

348
00:17:17.436 --> 00:17:19.438
of data within your agentic system.

349
00:17:19.838 --> 00:17:23.942
This is where data is stored, retrieved, and optimized as well.

350
00:17:24.243 --> 00:17:27.646
These are all the main system components associated with agent memory.

351
00:17:28.147 --> 00:17:31.450
But an agent memory core would be your database.

352
00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:35.054
This is where the most information and data

353
00:17:35.454 --> 00:17:37.556
would be stored and retrieved from

354
00:17:37.756 --> 00:17:39.958
in the entirety of your agentic system.

355
00:17:40.225 --> 00:17:43.128
Most of the data traffic we see in AI agents

356
00:17:43.128 --> 00:17:45.431
flow through an external memory

357
00:17:45.431 --> 00:17:47.833
which is backed by a database.

358
00:17:48.233 --> 00:17:51.737
An Agent Memory Core can be defined as this primary infrastructure

359
00:17:51.737 --> 00:17:55.441
that will see the most data traffic within your agentic system.

360
00:17:55.741 --> 00:17:59.044
It should be able to handle the storage of information,

361
00:17:59.044 --> 00:18:01.747
the retrieval and the optimization of information

362
00:18:01.747 --> 00:18:03.449
within the store itself.

363
00:18:03.749 --> 00:18:05.584
This is why we refer to

364
00:18:05.584 --> 00:18:07.719
the database as the Agent Memory Core.

365
00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:10.222
This is an important concept to

366
00:18:10.222 --> 00:18:12.524
take along with you through this course.
